---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "@/components/Code.astro";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/utils";
const snippet0 = `from power_pptx import Presentation

prs = Presentation("deck.pptx")
for slide in prs.slides:
    report = slide.lint()
    for issue in report.issues:
        print(issue.severity.value, issue)`;

const snippetOverflow = `from power_pptx.lint import TextOverflow

for issue in slide.lint().issues:
    if isinstance(issue, TextOverflow):
        issue.severity       # ERROR for a fixed-size frame, WARNING if it auto-sizes
        issue.ratio          # 3.0 - the text needs 3x the height available
        issue.dimension      # "height", or "width" for a line that cannot wrap
        issue.overflow_pt    # 43.2 - how far it runs past the frame
        issue.auto_size      # "NONE" | "SHAPE_TO_FIT_TEXT" | "TEXT_TO_FIT_SHAPE" | None
        issue.estimated      # True when the typeface is not installed here
        issue.row, issue.col # the cell, for an overflowing table row; else None`;

const snippet1 = `report = slide.lint()
report.auto_fix()          # mutates: clamps OffSlide, makes overflowing text fit,
                           # restacks contradicted layer declarations
report.auto_fix(dry_run=True)          # describe the fixes, change nothing
report.auto_fix(fix_grid_drift=True)   # also snap OffGridDrift (off by default)

# One call instead of lint -> fix -> re-lint
slide.tidy()
slide.tidy(fix_grid_drift=True)    # opt into grid snapping
slide.tidy(fix_layer_order=False)  # leave z-order alone`;

const cli = `power-pptx lint deck.pptx                         # report grouped by slide
power-pptx lint build/*.pptx --fail-on warning    # stricter gate
power-pptx lint deck.pptx --min-severity warning  # hide info-level issues
power-pptx lint deck.pptx --format json           # one JSON document
power-pptx lint deck.pptx --format sarif > lint.sarif   # code scanning
power-pptx lint deck.pptx --fix --output tidied.pptx    # slide.tidy(), then report
power-pptx lint deck.pptx --fix --in-place        # overwrite (opt-in only)`;

const cliOut = `$ power-pptx lint deck.pptx
deck.pptx: 3 issues on 1 of 1 slide (2 errors, 1 warning)

  Slide 1
    error    OffSlide      Shape 'Off card' extends beyond the right edge of the slide.
    error    TextOverflow  Shape 'Caption': text needs 3.0× the text frame height (43pt too tall).
    warning  TextOverflow  Shape 'Body': text needs 3.0× the text frame height (43pt too tall). The shape is set to resize to its text, so it grows when rendered or edited and its stored size is stale.
$ echo $?
1`;

const snippet2 = `from power_pptx.enum.shapes import MSO_SHAPE
from power_pptx.util import Inches

# A KPI card: backing panel, accent bar, value text, and a delta badge
# hanging off the top-right corner.
card = slide.shapes.add_shape(
    MSO_SHAPE.ROUNDED_RECTANGLE, Inches(0.8), Inches(1.5),
    Inches(3.4), Inches(2.0))
accent_bar = slide.shapes.add_shape(
    MSO_SHAPE.RECTANGLE, Inches(0.8), Inches(1.5), Inches(3.4), Inches(0.2))
value_box = slide.shapes.add_textbox(
    Inches(1.0), Inches(2.0), Inches(3.0), Inches(0.9))
badge = slide.shapes.add_shape(
    MSO_SHAPE.OVAL, Inches(3.6), Inches(1.2), Inches(1.0), Inches(0.6))

# 1. Group tag - n-ary and symmetric: everything sharing a non-empty
#    tag may overlap everything else in the tag.
slide.lint_group("kpi-1", card, accent_bar, value_box)
slide.lint_group_overlaps(card, accent_bar, value_box)  # auto-names it

# 2. Pairwise allowance - licenses exactly one pair, nothing else.
badge.allow_overlap_with(card)
badge.disallow_overlap_with(card)           # revoke
badge.overlap_allowances                    # frozenset[int] of shape ids

# 3. Layer hints - the only form that also asserts z-order.
card.layer = "card"
badge.layer_above = "card"`;

const snippet3 = `# 'badge' was added to the slide *before* 'card', so it is painted
# underneath the thing it claims to sit on top of.
card.layer = "card"
badge.layer_above = "card"

report = slide.lint()
report.has_errors        # True
report.auto_fix()
# ["Restacked 'Delta badge' above 'KPI card' to honour layer_above='card'."]`;

const snippet4 = `prs.lint_on_save = "off"      # default - no checks, no cost
prs.lint_on_save = "warn"     # log error-severity issues, still write
prs.lint_on_save = "raise"    # raise LintError instead of writing

prs.save("out.pptx")`;

const snippet5 = `from power_pptx.compose import from_spec

prs = from_spec({
    "slides": [{"layout": "title", "title": "Q4 Review"}],
    "lint": "raise",                  # raise | warn | off
})`;

const snippet6 = `from power_pptx import audit

report = audit(prs)
print(report.markdown())`;

---

<DocsLayout
  title="Lint & Audit"
  description="slide.lint(), issue types and severities, declaring intentional overlaps, auto_fix, tidy(), lint_on_save, and whole-deck audit()."
>
  <p class="lead">
    The layout linter is the last line of defence in the space-aware stack: it measures the slide
    and reports anything that would look broken in PowerPoint.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lint">slide.lint()</h2>
  <Code code={snippet0} />
  <p>
    Linting never changes the deck — nor do <code>audit(prs)</code> and
    <code>audit_accessibility(prs)</code> — and it scales: 800 text boxes lint in well under a
    second. <code>slide.lint(disable=[...])</code> and <code>min_severity=</code> skip the rules
    they rule out instead of running them and discarding the result, so they make linting faster
    too. Hidden slides are linted like any other.
  </p>

  <h2 id="issues">Issue types</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Default severity</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>TextOverflow</code></td><td>Text does not fit its frame or table row, measured with the metrics <code>fit_text</code> uses</td><td>error for a fixed-size frame; warning for an auto-size frame or a table row</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OffSlide</code></td><td>Shape extends past the slide bounds (rotated, and grown to its text)</td><td>error</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ShapeCollision</code></td><td>Unintentional overlap between shapes</td><td>warning; info for a small shape inside a large one, or a near-identical box</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>LayerOrderViolation</code></td><td>A shape declares <code>layer_above</code> but is drawn <em>below</em> that layer, so it will be hidden</td><td>error</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>InvalidGeometry</code></td><td>A shape has a negative width or height, which PowerPoint repairs; it is left out of the other checks</td><td>error</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>MinFontSize</code></td><td>A run is set below 9pt (after any stored shrink-on-overflow scale)</td><td>warning</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>LowContrast</code></td><td>Text on its backdrop is below WCAG AA, 4.5:1</td><td>warning</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OffGridDrift</code></td><td>An edge sits slightly off a grid line its siblings share</td><td>warning</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ZOrderAnomaly</code></td><td>An opaque filled shape is drawn over a shape it visually contains</td><td>warning</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>MasterPlaceholderCollision</code></td><td>A shape redraws an unused layout placeholder instead of filling it</td><td>warning</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>RaggedEdge</code></td><td>A filled panel stops short of the bottom edge the panels beside it share</td><td>warning</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>OffSlideShadow</code> and <code>ShapeCollisionShadow</code> are the same checks applied to
    shadow bleed, reported only with <code>slide.lint(include_effect_bleed=True)</code>. A rotated
    shape is checked by the area its rotated frame covers, and a connector by the line it is drawn
    along: it collides only with a shape that line runs into.
  </p>

  <h3 id="text-overflow">How TextOverflow measures</h3>
  <p>
    The linter lays text out with the same font metrics as <code>fit_text</code>: every run at its
    effective size and typeface (including what a placeholder inherits from its layout and
    master), wrapped inside the frame's margins where <code>fit_text</code> breaks lines — at
    spaces, after hyphens, dashes and URL slashes, between East Asian characters — and stacked at
    the frame's line and paragraph spacing. Text inside groups and table cells is measured too, and
    a frame <code>fit_text</code> has sized is never flagged.
  </p>
  <Code code={snippetOverflow} />
  <p>
    Severity follows who can repair the overflow. A fixed-size frame (<code>NONE</code>, or no
    setting) spills in every renderer, so it is an error. A frame that resizes to its text — what
    <code>add_textbox</code> and <code>add_text</code> create — a shrink-on-overflow frame whose
    stored scale is not enough, and a table row shorter than its text are warnings: a renderer
    adjusts them when the deck is opened or edited, so <code>lint_on_save = "raise"</code> does not
    reject them. A box that resizes to its text is checked by <code>OffSlide</code> and
    <code>ShapeCollision</code> at the size it will grow to, so one that grows off the slide is
    still an error. When a typeface is not installed on the machine running the linter the
    figures are estimates: the message says so and <code>estimated</code> is <code>True</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="autofix">auto_fix and tidy</h2>
  <Code code={snippet1} />
  <p>
    Auto-fix handles the issues that need no designer judgment, and returns a one-line
    description of each change:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>OffSlide</code> — moves the shape so the area it covers sits inside the slide,
      shrinking it first if it is larger than the slide (a picture or group proportionally).
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>TextOverflow</code> — makes the text fit the way the frame's own setting asks, never
      changing the text itself: a frame with no setting, or shrink-on-overflow, gets the font scale
      PowerPoint would store when it shrinks text itself; a frame that resizes to its text is
      resized; a <code>NONE</code> frame keeps its setting and has its font sizes reduced, as
      <code>fit_text</code> does; a table row is made taller. Text that would have to shrink below
      a quarter of its size is left alone and stays in the report.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>LayerOrderViolation</code> — restacks the shape that declared
      <code>layer_above</code>; geometry is untouched.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>OffGridDrift</code> — snapped onto the grid line only when you pass
      <code>fix_grid_drift=True</code>, to <code>auto_fix()</code> or <code>tidy()</code>, because an
      inferred grid can be wrong.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>ShapeCollision</code> is deliberately left alone — nudging shapes apart almost always
    breaks the design. If the overlap was intentional, say so instead. <code>LowContrast</code>,
    <code>MinFontSize</code>, <code>ZOrderAnomaly</code>, <code>MasterPlaceholderCollision</code>
    and <code>RaggedEdge</code> need a designer too. After a real (not dry-run) fix,
    <code>report.issues</code> holds what is left.
  </p>

  <h2 id="intentional-overlap">Declaring an intentional overlap</h2>
  <p>
    <code>ShapeCollision</code> is the noisiest rule, because deliberate layering — a badge on a
    KPI card, an accent bar on a panel — looks exactly like a copy-paste bug from a bounding box
    alone. Tell the linter what you meant and it stops guessing. Three ways, narrowest last:
  </p>
  <Code code={snippet2} />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Scope</th><th>Reach for it when</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>lint_group</code></td>
        <td>n-ary, symmetric</td>
        <td>Several shapes form one visual cluster and any of them may overlap any other</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>allow_overlap_with</code></td>
        <td>one pair</td>
        <td>
          Exactly one overlap is meant to be legal and you want the rest still policed. Both
          shapes must be on the same slide — allowances are keyed on a shape id, which repeats
          across a deck, so naming a shape from another slide raises <code>ValueError</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>layer</code> / <code>layer_above</code></td>
        <td>directional</td>
        <td>The stacking <em>order</em> is part of the design and you want it enforced, not merely tolerated</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    An allowance is written one-sided but read symmetrically: it takes only one of the pair to
    vouch for the overlap, so calling it on either shape is equivalent. Allowances accumulate
    rather than replace, and revoking one that was never granted is a no-op.
  </p>
  <p>
    Layer hints are the only form that can <strong>fail</strong>. Declaring
    <code>layer_above = "card"</code> asserts this shape is painted on top of every overlapping
    shape whose <code>layer</code> is <code>"card"</code>. If the shape tree says otherwise, you
    get a <code>LayerOrderViolation</code> at error severity, because the declaration records what
    you meant and the drawing order is what failed to deliver it:
  </p>
  <Code code={snippet3} />
  <p>
    A layer name describes a stratum of the design rather than one cluster, so any number of
    unrelated shapes may share it. Only overlapping pairs are checked — a layer declaration
    between shapes that never touch is inert, not wrong. All three forms round-trip through
    save/open in the shape's <code>cNvPr</code> element and are invisible to PowerPoint.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lint-on-save">Linting on save</h2>
  <p>
    Every <code>Presentation</code> carries a save-time gate, whatever built it:
  </p>
  <Code code={snippet4} />
  <p>
    Only <strong>error</strong>-severity issues count. The lint pass runs <em>before</em> anything
    is written, so <code>"raise"</code> never leaves a bad file on disk — it raises
    <code>power_pptx.exc.LintError</code> naming the offending slide indexes. <code>"warn"</code>
    logs on the <code>power_pptx.presentation</code> logger and writes the file anyway. The
    setting lives on the in-memory deck only; re-open the saved file and it is back to
    <code>"off"</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="from-spec">The from_spec lint gate</h2>
  <Code code={snippet5} />
  <p>
    Spec slides can also carry the intent declarations themselves — a <code>"shapes"</code> entry
    accepts <code>lint_group</code>, <code>allow_overlap_with</code>, <code>layer</code> and
    <code>layer_above</code>, so a generator declares deliberate layering at generation time and
    the built deck lints clean with no tagging pass. See
    <a href={withBase("/advanced/compose")}>Compose &amp; Templates</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cli">From the command line: <code>power-pptx lint</code></h2>
  <p>
    The installed <code>power-pptx</code> command checks saved decks without any Python — for a CI
    job that gates decks another job built, or a deck an agent handed back. It runs
    <code>slide.lint()</code> on every slide of every deck named:
  </p>
  <Code code={cli} lang="bash" />
  <Code code={cliOut} lang="text" />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Exit status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>No issue at or above the <code>--fail-on</code> level</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>At least one issue at or above the <code>--fail-on</code> level</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>A usage error, or a deck that could not be read or written</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>--fail-on {"{error,warning,never}"}</code>, default <code>error</code>. Info-level
      issues never fail the run. An unreadable deck exits <code>2</code> even with
      <code>never</code>, and the decks that could be read are still reported.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>--min-severity {"{info,warning,error}"}</code> drops lower issues from the report
      <em>and</em> from the exit status.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>--format json</code> writes one document; each slide entry is that slide's
      <code>report.to_dict()</code> plus its <code>index</code>, <code>number</code>,
      <code>title</code> and <code>hidden</code> flag. <code>--format sarif</code> is the linter's
      SARIF 2.1.0 output with one run per deck, ready for code-scanning upload.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>--fix</code> applies <code>slide.tidy()</code> and needs <code>--output PATH</code> or
      <code>--in-place</code>; it never overwrites the input otherwise. With several decks, or a
      path ending in a separator, <code>--output</code> is a directory. The report and the exit
      status then describe what is left after fixing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="audit">Whole-deck audit</h2>
  <Code code={snippet6} />
  <p>The audit aggregates, per deck:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>lint issues across every slide</li>
    <li>broken or missing pictures</li>
    <li>empty slides</li>
    <li>
      uncommon-font warnings (fonts recipients are unlikely to have), once per font per slide, for
      fonts named on runs, paragraphs and table cells and through theme references such as
      <code>+mn-lt</code>
    </li>
    <li>oversized-picture warnings (bloat)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>AuditReport.markdown()</code> formats the lot for a chat reply or CI log, and
    <code>to_dict()</code> / <code>to_json()</code> give the same data with a slide index on every
    lint issue.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
